## Enforcing Ownership at Compile Time

### The Core Rules

1. **Each value has exactly one owner** at any time
2. **You can have either**:
   - One mutable reference (`&mut T`)
   - Any number of immutable references (`&T`)
   - Never both simultaneously
3. **References must always be valid** (no dangling references)

### Borrow Checker Algorithm

```xiom
// The borrow checker tracks:
pub type BorrowState = {
  variables: HashMap<VarId, BorrowInfo>;
  loans: Vec<Loan>;
}

pub type Loan = {
  var: VarId;
  kind: LoanKind,  // Shared or Mutable
  region: Region,  // Where the loan is active
}

pub enum LoanKind { Shared, Mutable }
```

### Lifetime Analysis

```xiom
fn example() {
  let mut x = 5;         // x is owned
  let r1 = &x;           // Shared loan on x (region r1)
  let r2 = &x;           // Shared loan on x (region r2) — OK!
  io.println(r1, r2);    // Both loans active
  // r1, r2 loans end here

  let r3 = &mut x;       // Mutable loan on x — OK, no other loans active
  r3 = 10;               // Use mutable loan
  // r3 loan ends here
}
```

### NLL (Non-Lexical Lifetimes)

```xiom
fn nll_example() {
  let mut v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(1);

  let first = &v[0];     // Loan starts
  io.println(first);      // Use loan
  // Loan ends HERE (after last use), not at end of scope

  v.push(2);              // OK! Loan has ended
}
```

### Common Borrow Checker Errors

```xiom
// E201: Use after move
let x = Vec[Int]();
let y = x;         // x moved to y
x.push(1);         // ERROR: x was moved

// E202: Cannot borrow as mutable
let mut x = 5;
let r1 = &x;       // Shared borrow
let r2 = &mut x;   // ERROR: cannot borrow mutably while shared borrow exists

// E203: Cannot return reference to local
fn dangle() -> &Int {
  let x = 42;
  return &x;       // ERROR: x is dropped at end of function
}
```

### Polonius (Next-Gen Borrow Checker)

The next-generation borrow checker uses datalog-based analysis for more precise lifetimes:

```
// More patterns accepted by Polonius:
// - Conditional borrows (borrow in if/else branches)
// - More precise drop tracking
// - Better closure borrowing analysis
```

